/**
\defgroup chain_pair The chain rule for two variables in two variables
\brief The chain rule for the composition of two variables in two variables up to total order 6.

Include `<laplace/math/chain_pair.hpp>` and call \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_pair_dk "chain_rule_pair_dk()" as `laplace::math::chain_rule_pair_dk<K>(G, u0, u1, out)` to compute all partial derivatives of total order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ of \f$H(x_0, x_1) = G(u_0(x_0, x_1), u_1(x_0, x_1))\f$. Functions in this module are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos device execution. On the gcc reference suite, errors stay within 2.8 ulp on cancelling random derivatives and 26.2 ulp on non-linear coordinate maps, bounded at 40.0 ulp in automated tests.

## Definitions and array layout

Let \f$G \colon \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}\f$ be an outer function of \f$(u_0, u_1)\f$, and let \f$u_0, u_1 \colon \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}\f$ be two inner functions of \f$(x_0, x_1)\f$, forming \f$H(x_0, x_1) = G(u_0(x_0, x_1), u_1(x_0, x_1))\f$. Given partial derivatives of \f$G\f$ in \f$(u_0, u_1)\f$ and of \f$u_0, u_1\f$ in \f$(x_0, x_1)\f$ up to total order \f$K\f$, the function computes all partial derivatives of \f$H\f$ of total order \f$K\f$:
\f[
\frac{\partial^K H}{\partial x_0^{K-b} \partial x_1^b}, \quad b \in \{0, \dots, K\},
\f]
where \f$b\f$ is the number of derivatives in \f$x_1\f$ and \f$K - b\f$ is the number in \f$x_0\f$. Univariate composition is covered on \ref faa_di_bruno "Faà di Bruno's formula and partial Bell polynomials", where mixed partials factorize into products of univariate Bell sums only when each inner function depends on one variable alone. Here both \f$u_0\f$ and \f$u_1\f$ may depend simultaneously on both \f$x_0\f$ and \f$x_1\f$, so mixed partials do not factorize.

Input derivatives are packed contiguously so that a multi-index \f$(i, j)\f$ of total order \f$n = i + j\f$ sits at linear index
\f[
\frac{(i + j)(i + j + 1)}{2} + j,
\f]
ordered first by total order \f$n\f$ and then by the number of derivatives \f$j\f$ in the second variable. Within one order \f$n\f$, this matches the packing of a symmetric tensor of order \f$n\f$ in two variables, with the component having \f$j\f$ indices equal to 1 at relative offset \f$j\f$.

Array `G` stores \f$\partial^{i+j} G / \partial u_0^i \partial u_1^j\f$ in \f$(u_0, u_1)\f$ for \f$0 \le i + j \le K\f$. Array `u0` stores \f$\partial^{p+q} u_0 / \partial x_0^p \partial x_1^q\f$ in \f$(x_0, x_1)\f$ for \f$1 \le p + q \le K\f$, and array `u1` packs derivatives of \f$u_1\f$ in \f$(x_0, x_1)\f$ identically to `u0`. Output array `out` receives \f$\partial^K H / \partial x_0^{K-b} \partial x_1^b\f$ in `out[b]` for \f$b \in \{0, \dots, K\}\f$.

## Domain and preconditions

Template order \f$K\f$ has the unchecked precondition \f$1 \le K \le 6\f$; the header enforces no compile-time or run-time check outside this range. Arrays `G`, `u0`, and `u1` must each hold at least \f$(K + 1)(K + 2)/2\f$ doubles, and `out` must hold at least \f$K + 1\f$ doubles, as unchecked preconditions. The undifferentiated entries `u0[0]` and `u1[0]` are never read, and although `G[0]` is copied during initialization, it does not enter the recurrence for \f$K \ge 1\f$. Any NaN in a contributing derivative of `G`, `u0`, or `u1` propagates through the recurrence sums and products to the affected entries of `out`.

## Numerical method

Instead of multivariate Bell polynomials (Constantine and Savits, 1996), the module evaluates a bivariate Leibniz recurrence. Let \f$\gamma = (p, q) \in \mathbb{N}_0^2\f$ and \f$k = (i, j) \in \mathbb{N}_0^2\f$ be multi-indices for derivatives in \f$(x_0, x_1)\f$ and \f$(u_0, u_1)\f$, with \f$G_k = \partial^{i+j} G / \partial u_0^i \partial u_1^j\f$. Writing \f$D[\gamma][k] = \partial^\gamma (G_k \circ u)\f$ and differentiating once more in coordinate \f$x_m\f$ for \f$m \in \{0, 1\}\f$ by Leibniz's rule gives
\f[
D[\gamma + e_m][k] = \sum_{\alpha \le \gamma} \binom{\gamma}{\alpha} \sum_{r=0}^1 D[\alpha][k + e_r] \, \partial^{\gamma - \alpha + e_m} u_r,
\f]
starting from \f$D[0][k] = G_k\f$. Here \f$\alpha = (p', q')\f$ satisfies \f$0 \le p' \le p\f$ and \f$0 \le q' \le q\f$, \f$\binom{\gamma}{\alpha} = \binom{p}{p'} \binom{q}{q'}\f$, \f$e_m\f$ and \f$e_r\f$ are standard unit basis vectors in coordinate and argument directions, and \f$u_r\f$ denotes the \f$r\f$-th inner function. Because every coefficient in the recurrence is a positive binomial coefficient, the recurrence introduces no cancellation of its own; any cancellation comes from the input partial derivatives.

## Measured accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for error definitions, reference generation, and test bounds. Errors are measured in ulps of the largest reference component of total order \f$K\f$ against truncated bivariate Taylor series evaluated by `tools/reference/chain_pair.py` across 96 cases. Sixty cases draw random derivatives on \f$(2^{-8}, 2^8)\f$ with log-uniform magnitudes and random signs to induce cancellation, and 36 cases evaluate two non-linear bivariate coordinate maps with a log-density-shaped outer function.

On the gcc build, the random-derivative cases stay within 2.8 ulp across orders 1 to 6. The coordinate-map cases stay within 3.74 ulp for orders 1 to 5 and reach 26.2 ulp at order 6, with orders 1 to 4 and order 6 all attaining their maximum at the same point. Automated tests in `tests/math/test_chain_pair.cpp` check every case against a bound of 40.0 ulp on both gcc and CUDA, and verify order-6 evaluation inside a Kokkos kernel. See `docs/numerics.md` for per-order maxima.


## Example

The following example evaluates the order-2 composite derivatives of \f$H(x_0, x_1) = G(u_0(x_0, x_1), u_1(x_0, x_1))\f$ inside a `Kokkos::parallel_for` kernel:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/math/chain_pair.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void compute_pair_derivatives(Kokkos::View<double**>& out_derivs, int n) {
  constexpr int K = 2;
  constexpr int num_inputs = (K + 1) * (K + 2) / 2;  // 6 entries for K = 2
  constexpr int num_outputs = K + 1;                 // 3 entries for K = 2

  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_chain_pair", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    // Buffers with compile-time constant sizes
    double G[num_inputs] = {};
    double u0[num_inputs] = {};
    double u1[num_inputs] = {};

    // Partial derivatives of G in (u0, u1) up to order 2:
    // (i + j)*(i + j + 1)/2 + j
    G[1] = 2.0;    // dG/du0
    G[2] = -3.0;   // dG/du1
    G[3] = 5.0;    // d^2 G / du0^2
    G[4] = 7.0;    // d^2 G / du0 du1
    G[5] = -11.0;  // d^2 G / du1^2

    // Partial derivatives of u0 and u1 in (x0, x1) up to order 2:
    // element 0 is never read
    u0[1] = 0.5;   // du0/dx0
    u0[2] = 1.5;   // du0/dx1
    u1[1] = -0.25; // du1/dx0
    u1[2] = 2.0;   // du1/dx1

    double d[num_outputs];
    laplace::math::chain_rule_pair_dk<K>(G, u0, u1, d);

    for (int b = 0; b < num_outputs; ++b) {
      out_derivs(i, b) = d[b];
    }
  });
}
\endcode

## Module functions

Functions in this module are called as `laplace::math::NAME`.

- \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_pair_dk "chain_rule_pair_dk()" — Evaluates partial derivatives of \f$G(u_0(x), u_1(x))\f$ of total order \f$K\f$ in \f$x = (x_0, x_1)\f$ for \f$1 \le K \le 6\f$

## References

- G. Constantine and T. Savits (1996), A Multivariate Faa di Bruno Formula with Applications, Transactions of the American Mathematical Society, 348(2), 503–520. https://doi.org/10.1090/s0002-9947-96-01501-2
*/
